.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1155 PM PDT Sun Aug 2 2026
(Tonight through Tuesday)

Upper level ridging builds over the eastern Pacific
and towards California while upper level troughing pushes across
southern CA and the intermountain west. Tonight, weak offshore flow
is set to develop over the Bay Area, which will lead to weak warming
and drying and keep the marine layer shallow. Models show it to be
around 500ft with any stratus development being confined to the
Monterey Bay and perhaps into the northern Salinas Valley. An otter
eddy is expected to form, which may pull it in and out of sites
periodically, with clouds mixing out by mid morning. After that,
sunny skies will prevail for much of the region leading to another
hot day for interior locations. Highs will generally be in the 90s
to near 105, with the hottest temperatures being found across the
interior locations for the Bay Area and Central Coast. For those
closer to the coast, San Francisco Bay, and Monterey Bay, the marine
layer should help keep temperatures manageable though the
gradient will be tight from the coast to just a few miles inland.
Look for highs ranging from the low to mid 60s to to mid 80s.
Widespread Moderate HeatRisk is expected across the interior
North Bay Mountains, the interior East Bay Valleys, the East Bay
Hills, the Santa Clara Valley, and the Santa Clara Hills. A Heat
Advisory has been hoisted for these conditions, which will run
from 10AM - 10PM Monday.

Here are some friendly reminders on how to beat the heat:

* Stay hydrated and drink plenty of fluids.
* Wear lightweight, light-colored clothing.
* Reduce time spent outdoors or stay in the shade.
* Never leave people or pets unattended in vehicles.
* Use sunscreen if going to the coast or the pool.

