.AVIATION...
(18Z TAFS)
Issued at 1133 AM PDT Mon Aug 3 2026

Current satellite imagery shows stratus receding to the coastline.
Widespread VFR conditions prevail through the day as onshore winds
increase to around 10-15 kt before easing into the evening. We had a
bit of a disorganized southerly surge of marine stratus that not
only brought IFR-LIFR ceilings to the coastline, but also south to
southwesterly winds. We can anticipate another southerly surge to
happen again tonight, though there is low to medium confidence on
the exact arrival of marine stratus and how far inland it will
perturb. Highest confidence for IFR ceilings, intermittently LIFR,
at HAF and the Monterey Bay terminals. High confidence SJC and LVK
will remain VFR through the TAF period. Low to medium confidence for
marine stratus to reach North Bay terminals, though TAFs hint at the
potential with SCT decks.

Vicinity of SFO...VFR with moderate onshore breezes into the
evening. Medium confidence on VFR conditions through the TAF period.
As described above, there is uncertainty on whether the southerly
surge of stratus will push through the Golden Gate and inland. If
the stratus makes it through the Golden Gate, it is possible for IFR
ceilings to reach OAK and SFO around the time of the SCT decks.
Leaned more on the optimistic side for this TAF period as the surge
was rather disorganized this morning and likely will be again
tomorrow morning.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...Onshore winds follow their usual diurnal
pattern with VFR conditions prevailing. As mentioned previously, the
southerly surge of marine stratus will return overnight with SW
winds likely for MRY. SNS will remain more NW. Low-end IFR ceilings
will impact SNS first and then MRY between 06-10Z. Winds should
remain light overnight, but there is the potential for visibility
impacts around 4-6SM due to coastal drizzle/fog. Ceilings may also
intermittently drop down to LIFR given how compressed the marine
layer will be.

