.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 1200 PM PDT Sun Aug 2 2026

Near critical fire weather conditions are expected through Monday
and after a period of elevated fire weather conditions, potentially
return later in the week. Temperatures remain around 10 to 15
degrees above normal across the interior through early next week
with highs in the upper 80s to 90s. No major changes to the
forecasted dry conditions. Daytime humidity retention remains poor
across the interior (20-30%) with humidity values dropping into
the 10s across the interior Central Coast, East Bay Hills/Valleys,
and the higher elevations. Portions of the interior Central Coast
may see daytime humidity values drop into the single digits
through the next couple of days. Overnight RH recovery remains
poor at locations above 2,000 feet with values between 25 to 40%
expected with some favored locations struggling to reach even 25%.
This will be particularly true across the Mayacamas, highest
peaks of the East Bay Hills, the Diablo Range, the Santa Cruz
Mountains, the Santa Lucia and Gabilan Ranges. Nocturnal drying
(rapid decrease in RH through the pre- dawn hours) may also
transpire which will increase the burn period or time in which
fire behavior may remain active. Energy Release Component (ERC)
values are projected to climb above the the percentile across the
interior Central Coast, Mid Coast to Mendocino (North Bay), and
Diablo- Santa Cruz (Diablo Mountains and Santa Cruz Mountains)
zones within the next two days. Diurnally breezy onshore winds are
expected along the coast, mountain gaps and passes, and along the
ridgelines each afternoon/evening. In addition, a burst of
offshore wind is forecast tonight into Monday morning for the SF
Bay Area and the North Bay, increasing the risk for nocturnal
drying events especially in downslope areas. With near critically
dry conditions and above normal temperatures expected, individuals
across these areas are advised to avoid activities that may spark
a fire. Fire crews are urged to exercise extra caution when
executing any prescribed burns, especially where fuels,
topography, and weather align.

